Adam the Namer

Pantheon role: Name what people can recognize, remember, and use.
Moneybin entry: The Art of Naming, a workshop for testing what a name changes.

Pantheon

Adam takes his cue from the Genesis scene in which the animals are brought before him and he gives them names. In the Pantheon, his work begins with an encounter. Someone brings him a moment they can describe but have trouble calling back. Adam listens for the detail that keeps recurring and gives it a name someone else could use.

> The Sphinx asks, “What did the silence tell you?”  
> Adam asks, “Which silence are we talking about?”  
> “The one after everyone thought the room had gone quiet.”  
> “Then call it the Second Silence. Tell me where else you hear it.”

The mechanic who hears a faint click after the engine stops and the listener who hears one voice after the song ends can both recognize a second silence. A different name, What He Was Waiting For, keeps the mechanic’s trained attention in view. Preserve the scene alongside the name so later tellers can recover either reading.

How Adam works

1. Keep the encounter. Write down what happened in plain language, with enough detail for someone else to picture it.
2. Choose what to bring forward. A name can highlight an action, a feeling, a cause, a relationship, or the audience being invited in.
3. Try several names on the same encounter. Include one plain description as a reference.
4. Listen to what the name makes people expect. Ask what they think it means and where else they’d use it before giving your definition.
5. Keep the name provisional. Record who uses it, where it travels, and when it produces a misleading inference. Revisit the original encounter when the label drifts.

Handoff: Encounter → candidate name → intended meaning → audience interpretation → new example → revision.

Moneybin · The Art of Naming

A repeatable naming workshop for a product, practice, format, or recurring experience. Bring one thing and three candidate names. Keep the description or clip identical and give each tester one version before comparing reactions.

|Name                |What it brings forward         |Expectation to check                   |
|--------------------|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------|
|90/90 hip stretch   |The movement itself            |“I know roughly what I’ll do.”         |
|Pigeon-inspired flow|The yoga lineage and style     |“I’ll be guided through a sequence.”   |
|Tactical Mobility   |A performance-oriented identity|“This is training for a physical task.”|

Ask each person What do you expect to happen? Who do you picture doing it? Where else would you use that name? For a small test, aim to collect the actual answers verbatim. Treat the results as clues for revision.

Wild Hawg application: Show the same clip of somebody juggling four drinks on the way out the door. Test Too Many Drinks, The Beverage Goblin, and The Fourth Drink. If people who hear The Beverage Goblin can supply their own scene without prompting, the character name is doing more than describing this clip. They can use it to point at a recurring experience.

Offer: A naming workshop delivered as three tested candidates, what each one leads people to expect, and one recommended name with an example of how to use it.

Decision rule: Favor the name people remember and use accurately in a new situation. Keep alternate names when they capture a different aspect worth preserving.